arm64: dts: rockchip: rk356x: clock the PIPE GRF so a kexec'd kernel can write it

rockchip_grf_init (drivers/soc/rockchip/grf.c) is a postcore_initcall that
writes three USB3-OTG bits into the rk3566 PIPE GRF at 0xfdc50000
("rockchip,rk3566-pipe-grf").  That register block sits on pclk_pipe.  On a
board with no PCIe, SATA or USB3 consumer nothing holds that clock, so the
running kernel gates it as unused at late init; U-Boot hands a cold boot
with it running, which is why cold boots never notice.

A kexec'd kernel inherits the previous kernel's clock tree.  Its
rockchip_grf_init then writes into an unclocked APB block, the access never
completes, and the boot stalls silently 0.12 s in -- the last line is the
DMA pool preallocation, and a minute later the workqueue watchdog reports a
lockup on whichever CPU ran init.  Seen three times on a Pine64 PineNote
(RK3566, 7.1.8) before a kexec with initcall_blacklist=rockchip_grf_init
booted all the way; clk_summary on the running kernel shows pclk_pipe at
enable count 0 while the pipe power domain is on.

Give the pipegrf syscon its clock.  syscon_node_to_regmap() registers a
"syscon"-compatible node with resource checking, which attaches the node's
first clock to the regmap and enables it around every access, so the GRF
init clocks the block itself before writing -- whatever the previous
kernel left behind.  The binding (soc/rockchip/grf.yaml) would want
`clocks` allowed for the pipe-grf compatibles alongside this.

wilkbook: doc/upstream-register.md item 22; doc/update-path.md "Glass
notes" (2026-09-02).

--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/rockchip/rk356x-base.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/rockchip/rk356x-base.dtsi
@@ -375,6 +375,7 @@
 
 	pipegrf: syscon@fdc50000 {
 		reg = <0x0 0xfdc50000 0x0 0x1000>;
+		clocks = <&cru PCLK_PIPE>;
 	};
 
 	grf: syscon@fdc60000 {
